lessons.content_markdown:
## Writing Your First Test

Every Playwright test file exports one or more `test()` blocks. Each test gets an isolated `page` (and `context`) fixture automatically — no manual setup/teardown required.

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('homepage has correct title', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://playwright.dev');
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Playwright/);
});
```

Run it with:
```bash
npx playwright test
```

And view the HTML report:
```bash
npx playwright show-report
```

Notice there's no `driver.quit()`, no explicit waits, and no boilerplate — Playwright Test handles the lifecycle for you via **fixtures**, which we'll cover in depth later.

