lessons.content_markdown:
## Filtering Locators

Real apps rarely have one unique element — product grids, table rows, and notification lists all repeat. Playwright locators are **lazy and chainable**, letting you narrow scope precisely.

```ts
// Find the row containing 'Wireless Mouse', then click its delete button
await page.getByRole('row', { name: 'Wireless Mouse' })
  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' })
  .click();

// Filter by having text
const product = page.getByTestId('product-card').filter({ hasText: 'In Stock' });
await expect(product).toHaveCount(3);

// Nth element (use sparingly — prefer semantic filters when possible)
await page.getByTestId('product-card').nth(0).click();
```

Chaining locators doesn't query the DOM immediately — it builds a description that's resolved lazily at the moment of the action, which is part of why it plays so well with auto-waiting.

lessons.starter_code:
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('filter and act on a specific row', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/inventory');
  const row = page.getByRole('row', { name: 'Wireless Mouse' });
  await row.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('row', { name: 'Wireless Mouse' })).toHaveCount(0);
});

