lessons.content_markdown:
## `expect(locator)` vs `expect(value)`

Playwright's `expect` has two modes:

- **Web-first assertions** (`expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `toHaveText()`, `toHaveCount()`) — these **retry automatically** for the timeout duration, matching the async nature of a real UI.
- **Generic assertions** (`expect(value).toBe()`) — a single synchronous check, no retrying.

```ts
// ✅ Retries until the toast appears or times out
await expect(page.getByText('Saved successfully')).toBeVisible();

// ❌ No retry — will fail immediately if the value isn't ready yet
const text = await page.getByText('Saved successfully').textContent();
expect(text).toBe('Saved successfully');
```

Always prefer web-first assertions for anything tied to UI state — they are the single biggest reason Playwright suites are less flaky than older frameworks.

lessons.starter_code:
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('web-first assertions retry until true', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByText('Saved successfully')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toHaveText(/saved/i);
});

