lessons.content_markdown:
## `page.route()`

Intercept and modify any network request/response:

```ts
await page.route('**/api/user', route =>
  route.fulfill({
    status: 200,
    contentType: 'application/json',
    body: JSON.stringify({ id: 1, plan: 'lifetime' }),
  })
);

await page.goto('/dashboard');
await expect(page.getByText('Lifetime Member')).toBeVisible();
```

You can also simulate failures and slow networks:
```ts
await page.route('**/api/payments', route => route.abort());
// or
await page.route('**/*', route =>
  new Promise(res => setTimeout(() => res(route.continue()), 3000))
);
```

This is invaluable for testing error states, loading skeletons, and edge cases that are hard or impossible to trigger against a real backend.

